   "reasoning": "(a) About 8-9 days until the window opens (Nov 26-27), and the data is known by Nov 28 or shortly after.\n\n(b) If nothing changed, the observed maximum would equal the predicted high of 6.84 ft on Nov 26, or about 6.9 ft allowing for the 6-minute peak. Nov 27's predicted high is 6.5 ft.\n\n(c) If the current trend continued, residuals of about 0.8 to 1.4 ft would put the maximum at roughly 7.7 to 8.2 ft. The Nov 13 residual was about 1 ft. The October residual was 12-17 inches. Late November residuals may fade as the Kelvin wave passes and the El Ni\u00f1o's sea-level anomaly persists. A typical El Ni\u00f1o anomaly of 0.5-1.0 ft is plausible, plus a possible storm surge. The Nov 25 high of 6.96 ft is outside the window.\n\n(d) The City of Newport Beach and NWS expect elevated levels and possible flooding. The Newport Beach warning of 12 inches above the October record of 8.4 ft looks exaggerated. NWS quoted total water levels about 1.3-1.4 ft above normal.\n\n(e) Low scenario: the residual collapses to around 0.2-0.4 ft because the Kelvin wave passes, the sea surface cools, and the weather is calm with a high-pressure system. The maximum would be about 7.0-7.2 ft. Missing data would lead to annulment, but that is not a numeric outcome.\n\n(f) High scenario: a large storm with strong swell and low pressure coincides with the Nov 26 morning high. A residual of 1.5 ft or more would push the maximum to 8.4-8.7 ft, above the October record.\n\nMy central estimate is a residual of about 0.8 ft on top of 6.84 ft, giving roughly 7.65 ft. The 6-minute maximum also includes wave-induced noise, which adds a little. Scripps Pier is sheltered somewhat, but it records swell effects. The window maximum could also fall at a different time than the predicted high, though the Nov 26 high is the larger of the two.\n\nPercentiles:\n\nPercentile 10: 6.95\nPercentile 20: 7.2\nPercentile 40: 7.5\nPercentile 60: 7.75\nPercentile 80: 8.05\nPercentile 90: 8.3"
